{"api_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/triton-lang/triton/issues/9230","collected_at":"2026-09-27T21:57:14.770924+00:00","comments":[{"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","body":"> Is this behavior expected (output buffer reuse between candidate runs)?\n\nYes this is expected behavior. The autotuner just calls your kernel with the provided arguments multiple times to run the benchmarks. It's up to the user to ensure that these configs are well tested and produce the same solution. \n\n> Should Triton warn/error when autotuned kernels can leave parts of outputs unwritten unless reset_to_zero / restore_value is specified?\n\nThe autotuner doesn't really know what tensors are even outputs, so this wouldn't be very practical. Perhaps this could be added as an additional debug mode check, but nobody on our side is looking into this.\n\n> This possible to add this restore_value here:\n\nThat kernel doesn't initialize the accumulator from C, so shouldn't need `restore_value`.","created_at":"2026-02-02T17:22:49Z","html_url":"https://github.com/triton-lang/triton/issues/9230#issuecomment-3836614387","id":3836614387,"issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/triton-lang/triton/issues/9230","minimized":null,"node_id":"IC_kwDOAWaHXs7krhbz","performed_via_github_app":null,"pin":null,"reactions":{"+1":0,"-1":0,"confused":0,"eyes":0,"heart":0,"hooray":0,"laugh":0,"rocket":0,"total_count":0,"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/triton-lang/triton/issues/comments/3836614387/reactions"},"updated_at":"2026-02-02T17:22:49Z","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/triton-lang/triton/issues/comments/3836614387","user":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/13238737?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/peterbell10/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/peterbell10/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/peterbell10/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/peterbell10/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/peterbell10","id":13238737,"login":"peterbell10","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzMjM4NzM3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/peterbell10/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/peterbell10/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/peterbell10/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/peterbell10/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/peterbell10/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/peterbell10","user_view_type":"public"}}],"issue":{"active_lock_reason":null,"assignee":null,"assignees":[],"author_association":"NONE","body":"### Describe the bug\n`@triton.autotune` evaluates multiple configs using the same output buffer. If different configs write to different (disjoint) regions of the output, the final output after autotune can contain a mixture of writes from multiple candidate runs. This can mask correctness bugs. running with a single config fails (as expected), but running with multiple configs can pass because another config’s candidate run filled the missing part of the output.\n\nThis is risky because it can lead to silent correctness issues and CI can miss regressions when autotune is enabled.\n\n### Reproduction script\n```python\nimport os\nimport torch\nimport triton\nimport triton.language as tl\n\ndef get_configs():\n    configs = [\n        triton.Config({'OFFSET': 0, 'ELEMENTS': 512}),\n        triton.Config({'OFFSET': 512, 'ELEMENTS': 512})\n    ]\n    repro = int(os.environ.get('REPRO', '0'))\n    return configs if repro else configs[:1]\n\n@triton.autotune(\n    configs=get_configs(),\n    key=[],\n)\n@triton.jit\ndef add_kernel(\n    x_ptr,\n    y_ptr,\n    output_ptr,\n    OFFSET: tl.constexpr,\n    ELEMENTS: tl.constexpr\n):\n    offsets = tl.arange(OFFSET, ELEMENTS + OFFSET)\n    x = tl.load(x_ptr + offsets)\n    y = tl.load(y_ptr + offsets)\n    output = x + y\n    tl.store(output_ptr + offsets, output)\n\ndef get_triton_result():\n    x = torch.ones(1024, device='xpu')\n    y = torch.ones(1024, device='xpu')\n    output = torch.empty(1024, device='xpu')\n    add_kernel[(1,)](x, y, output)\n    return output\n\ndef get_torch_result():\n    x = torch.ones(1024, device='xpu')\n    y = torch.ones(1024, device='xpu')\n    output = x + y\n    return output\n\ntriton_output = get_triton_result()\ntorch_output = get_torch_result()\ntorch.testing.assert_close(triton_output, torch_output)\n```\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nRun with a single config (expected failure, because only the first half `[0:512]` is written):\n\n```bash\nREPRO=0 python repro.py\n```\nRun with both configs enabled (autotune evaluates both candidates):\n\n```bash\nREPRO=1 python repro.py\n```\n\n### Suggested solution\n\nTo prevent correctness masking, outputs should be reset/restored between candidate runs.:\n\n```python\n@triton.autotune(\n    configs=get_configs(),\n    key=[],\n    reset_to_zero=['output_ptr'],\n)\n```\n\n### Questions \n- Is this behavior expected (output buffer reuse between candidate runs)?\n- Should Triton warn/error when autotuned kernels can leave parts of outputs unwritten unless `reset_to_zero` / `restore_value` is specified?\n- Would it make sense to provide a “correctness-safe autotune” mode that restores outputs by default?\n- This possible to add this restore_value here:\nhttps://github.com/triton-lang/triton/blob/20251a31b20b8189a673708d3a9c458854db2fb5/python/tutorials/03-matrix-multiplication.py#L228-L235\n\n### Environment details\n\nTriton: latest","closed_at":"2026-02-02T17:22:49Z","closed_by":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/13238737?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/peterbell10/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/peterbell10/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/peterbell10/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/peterbell10/gists{/gist_id